  {
    id:'opqb-t21-1', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'A 25-year-old woman presents with ptosis, diplopia, and fatigability of muscles by the end of the day. She also reports episodes of difficulty speaking after long conversations and difficulty swallowing, especially with solid food. Which of the following additional symptoms is most typically associated with this patient?',
    options:['Dysarthria and dysphagia','CRVO','Mask-like facies and cogwheel rigidity','Retinal Detachment'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:'The vignette is myasthenia gravis: ptosis worse at the end of the day, diplopia and fatigability in a young woman. The book\'s myasthenia box makes every element explicit — "The most important feature is fatigability", ptosis that is "worse at the end of the day and least on awakening", the disease "more common in women < 40", and among the other manifestations "Difficulty with swallowing (dysphagia)", "Difficulty with speaking (dysarthria)" and difficulty with chewing (ophthalmology.pdf p.185). Note that the stem has already described difficulty speaking and swallowing, so the key restates the patient\'s own findings in medical terms — the question is really a vocabulary check. The lecture teaches the same disease pictorially: five slides of myasthenia, with the fatigable ptosis and the red-lettered "Any pattern" of extraocular weakness, meaning myasthenia can imitate any nerve or muscle palsy (L30) Squint II, sl.17–21). CRVO is a sudden painless retinal vascular event of older, vasculopathic patients — nothing to do with a neuromuscular junction disorder. Mask-like facies with cogwheel rigidity is the facial hypomimia of parkinsonism, not myasthenia (not taken from the course material); the myasthenic face is different — the book calls lack of facial expression plus ptosis "myopathic facies" (ophthalmology.pdf p.185). Retinal detachment presents with flashes, floaters and a field curtain, not fatigable weakness. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Recognise the full symptom set of myasthenia gravis — fatigable ptosis, diplopia, dysarthria, dysphagia — and separate its facies from parkinsonism.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.133, Q1 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: ophthalmology.pdf p.185; L30) Squint II sl.17–21.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-2', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'Most common complication of Central Retinal Vein Occlusion (CRVO) is:',
    options:['Retinal detachment','Cystoid macular edema','Neovascular glaucoma','Vitreous hemorrhage'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'Macular oedema is the complication the retinal vascular lecture keeps returning to. "Chronic macular oedema" is printed among the features of non-ischaemic CRVO — the commoner form of the disease — and in the vein-occlusion pages it is named as the "most common cause of persistent poor VA" after occlusion, with its own management slide (wait 6–12 weeks, perform FA, consider laser only if macular perfusion is good) (L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders). The explicit ranking of macular oedema as the single most common complication of CRVO specifically is not taken from the course material, but it follows from those slides. Neovascular glaucoma is the feared complication of the ISCHAEMIC form — the lecture prints "Rubeosis irides in 50%" for ischaemic CRVO, which is why that subtype needs monthly review and panretinal photocoagulation once new vessels appear — but ischaemic CRVO is the minority of cases, so rubeotic glaucoma is the most dreaded complication, not the most common. Vitreous haemorrhage can follow neovascularisation but is downstream of it and less frequent still. Retinal detachment is not a printed complication of vein occlusion at all. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Rank macular oedema as the common complication of CRVO and neovascular glaucoma as the dreaded complication of its ischaemic subtype.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.133, Q2 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders (non-ischaemic CRVO signs; chronic macular oedema management; rubeosis in 50% of ischaemic CRVO).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-3', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract-astig',
    stem:'An old man is unable to see near objects such as books and his mobile phone. What is the appropriate treatment?',
    options:['Minus (–) glasses','Plus (+) glasses','Cylindrical glasses','No treatment is needed'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'Failing near vision with preserved distance vision in an elderly patient is presbyopia — the refraction deck glosses it as "Poor near vision as an aging process" (L7,8) Refractive errors). Its treatment is exactly the key: a spherical plus lens addition to the far-vision refraction, dispensed as reading glasses, bifocals or progressives (L7,8 sl.81), and the deck\'s correction tree assigns "Convex (Plus lenses)" to hyperopia and presbyopia. Minus (concave) lenses sit on the other branch of that same tree — they correct myopia, where the far point is closer than infinity, and would make a presbyope\'s near vision worse, not better. Cylindrical lenses correct astigmatism, the error where the optical system forms more than one focus; nothing in the stem suggests a meridional error. "No treatment is needed" is wrong because presbyopia is fully and simply correctable — leaving a man unable to read his phone untreated when a pair of reading glasses restores near function is not an option. This question is a within-section twin of Q16, which tests the same fact against a partly different option menu; both are transcribed. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Treat presbyopia with a plus-lens near addition and assign minus and cylindrical lenses to their own refractive errors.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.133, Q3 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L7,8) Refractive errors sl.81 (plus addition), correction-tree slide, presbyopia gloss.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-4', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:'In thyroid eye disease, what is the most commonly limited eye movement?',
    options:['Elevation','Depression','Adduction','Abduction'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:'Thyroid eye disease limits motility by restriction, not paralysis: the orbit lecture files it under "Restrictive myopathy (e.g. thyroid eye disease)" in its list of motility-defect mechanisms, distinct from ocular motor nerve involvement, and devotes its own slide run to the disease (L10) orbit, sl.15–19). The muscle most frequently infiltrated and fibrosed is the INFERIOR RECTUS; a tight, fibrotic inferior rectus tethers the globe and mechanically prevents it rotating upward, so the most commonly limited movement is ELEVATION — the eye cannot look up, and forced upgaze may even raise the intraocular pressure. The classical order of muscle involvement (inferior rectus, then medial rectus, then superior rectus, then lateral rectus — the "I\'M SLow" sequence) is not printed in the lecture or the cached orbit chapter and is not taken from the course material. Depression is the action of the inferior rectus itself and is preserved — the fibrosed muscle still pulls down; the defect is in the opposite direction to the tight muscle. Adduction fails when the LATERAL rectus is tight, and abduction when the MEDIAL rectus is tight — the medial rectus is the second commonest muscle involved, which makes abduction the runner-up answer, but elevation remains the classic and commonest deficit. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Explain why a fibrotic inferior rectus makes elevation the most commonly limited movement in thyroid eye disease.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.133, Q4 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L10) orbit (restrictive myopathy list; TED slides 15–19).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-5', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'What is the most common risk factor for rhegmatogenous retinal detachment?',
    options:['Diabetes mellitus','Lattice degeneration','Hypertension','Cataract surgery'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'Rhegmatogenous detachment is the most common type of retinal detachment and needs a retinal break: vitreous fluid seeps through a tear and pushes the retina off its blood supply, with printed common causes of "Aging (vitreous shrinkage), high myopia (nearsightedness), or eye trauma" (L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations, sl.6). The keyed risk factor, lattice degeneration, is the classic predisposing peripheral retinal degeneration — patches of retinal thinning with atrophic holes at which tears form, present in a large share of eyes that go on to rhegmatogenous detachment; the lecture itself never teaches it (the "degenerations" half of the deck was not delivered and lattice appears only on its own not-covered list), so the fact is not taken from the course material. The bank prints the option lowercase ("lattice degeneration"). Diabetes mellitus is the wrong mechanism entirely: advanced diabetic retinopathy is "THE MOST COMMON CAUSE" of TRACTIONAL detachment, where contracting fibrovascular membranes pull the retina off without any initial tear (L20 sl.7) — a different type from the rhegmatogenous form the stem names. Hypertension is not a printed cause of any detachment type. Cataract surgery is a genuine risk factor for rhegmatogenous detachment — aphakic and pseudophakic eyes detach more often (not taken from the course material) — but it ranks below lattice degeneration in the bank\'s keying, and no course source ranks the two. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Name lattice degeneration as the predisposing peripheral degeneration for rhegmatogenous detachment and assign diabetes to the tractional type.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.133, Q5 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations sl.6–7.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-6', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'A patient present with a herpetic corneal ulcer. What is the most common complication?',
    options:['Corneal perforation','Recurrence','Secondary bacterial infection','Glaucoma'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'Recurrence is the defining behaviour of herpetic keratitis. Herpes simplex virus establishes latency in the trigeminal ganglion and reactivates; the cornea lecture prints "Recurrent episodes" among the disease\'s features, and the strongest evidence of how central recurrence is to its natural history is that the lecture\'s stated prophylaxis targets it directly: "Prophylactic oral aciclovir 400 mg twice daily reduces recurrence rate by ~50%" (L15,16) Cornea). A drug regimen exists specifically to halve the recurrence rate — nothing comparable is printed for any of the distractors. Each recurrence of the dendritic ulcer (the branching epithelial lesion with terminal buds that stains with fluorescein) adds stromal scarring and corneal anaesthesia, which is why recurrent disease is also the route to the ulcer\'s long-term visual damage. Corneal perforation is a complication of deep or neglected ulcers of any cause but is uncommon in epithelial dendritic disease. Secondary bacterial infection can supervene on any epithelial defect yet is not the characteristic complication of herpes. Glaucoma is associated with herpetic UVEITIS and trabeculitis rather than with the corneal ulcer itself (not taken from the course material). Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Identify recurrence as the hallmark complication of herpetic keratitis and recall the aciclovir 400 mg twice-daily prophylaxis that halves it.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.133, Q6 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L15,16) Cornea (recurrent episodes; prophylactic aciclovir; dendritic ulcer).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-7', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'A patient present with a history of severe blunt trauma to the eye, suspected rupture of the eyeball. What is the immediate management?',
    options:['Administer topical antibiotic','Withhold food and drink','Apply pressure patch','Perform immediate ocular massage'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'A ruptured globe is an open-globe injury, which the trauma lecture calls the "MOST DEVASTATING of all eye injuries" and instructs must be ruled out BEFORE any detailed history or focused examination (L25) Trauma I, sl.49–50, with peaked pupil, abnormal anterior chamber depth, bullous subconjunctival haemorrhage and a positive Seidel test as its warning signs). Its definitive treatment is surgical: an open-globe injury — rupture or full-thickness laceration — is "REPAIRED WITH SUTURES" (L25 sl.43–45), and that repair happens urgently under general anaesthesia. The immediate ward-level management is therefore to keep the patient nil by mouth — withholding food and drink is what makes prompt general anaesthesia possible, and it is the only option here that does not threaten the eye (the fasting-for-anaesthesia rationale is not taken from the course material). Every distractor applies pressure or manipulation to an eye whose wall is open: a pressure patch and ocular massage both squeeze the globe and risk extruding intraocular contents through the wound — massage is the manoeuvre for central retinal artery occlusion, catastrophically misapplied here — and instilling topical medication involves pressing on the lids and delivers drops into an open wound; a rigid shield without pressure, not a patch, is what such an eye is given (shield detail not taken from the course material). Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Manage a suspected globe rupture by keeping the patient fasting for urgent surgical repair and by never pressing on the eye.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.133, Q7 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L25) Trauma I sl.43–45, 49–50.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-8', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'A patient present with sudden, painless loss of vision in one eye. Fundus examination shows a pale retina with a cherry-red spot at the macula. What is the initial management?',
    options:['Intravitreal steroid injection','Ocular massage','Laser photocoagulation','Topical beta-blocker'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'Sudden painless monocular visual loss with a pale retina and a "cherry-red spot at macula" is central retinal artery occlusion — the retinal vascular lecture prints exactly those signs, the surrounding infarcted retina turning white and opaque while the thin foveal centre keeps transmitting the choroidal red, with segmentation ("cattle-trucking") of the arterial blood column (L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders). CRAO is the ophthalmic stroke: retinal survival is measured in hours, so management is immediate and aims to move the obstruction on. Ocular massage — intermittent firm pressure on the globe through closed lids — is the classic first manoeuvre: the pressure swings dilate the retinal arterioles and may dislodge the embolus into a more peripheral branch. The acute-visual-loss lecture\'s CRAO management slides are blank in the cached deck, so the massage manoeuvre and its rationale are not taken from the course material. Intravitreal steroid treats macular oedema in vein occlusion and uveitis — there is no oedema to treat in an infarcted retina, and no time. Laser photocoagulation belongs to the proliferative aftermath of ISCHAEMIC VEIN occlusion and diabetic retinopathy, not to the acute arterial event. A topical beta-blocker lowers intraocular pressure far too slowly and weakly to matter here; when pressure reduction is wanted acutely in CRAO it is sought with faster systemic agents (see Q26 in this exam). Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Diagnose CRAO from the pale retina with a cherry-red spot and begin ocular massage immediately to try to dislodge the embolus.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.133, Q8 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders (CRAO signs); L31,32) Acute visual Loss (management slides blank in the cached deck).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-9', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'A patient present with a chalazion on the upper eyelid. What is the preferred surgical approach for incision?',
    options:['Skin incision','Palpebral incision','Lateral canthal incision','Corneal incision'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'A chalazion is a chronic lipogranuloma of a meibomian gland buried in the tarsal plate, and the operation for it is incision and curettage performed from the CONJUNCTIVAL side of the lid — the book states that the persistent chalazion is treated by "incision and curettage through the conjunctival side" (ophthalmology.pdf p.73), and the eyelid lecture shows the operation photographically: the lid everted in a clamp and the granuloma incised and curetted through the tarsal conjunctival surface (L5) Eyelid disease, sl.18–19). That trans-conjunctival, trans-tarsal route is what this bank\'s "palpebral incision" denotes — the cut is made on the inner (palpebral conjunctival) face of the lid, where it drains the gland directly and heals without a visible scar; on that surface the incision is made vertically so as to run parallel with the meibomian glands (orientation detail not taken from the course material). A skin incision is reserved for the minority of chalazia pointing anteriorly through the skin, and on the standard lesion it trades an invisible scar for a visible one. A lateral canthal incision (canthotomy) is an orbital-access and lid-tightening approach with no relation to a tarsal cyst. A corneal incision belongs to intraocular surgery and has no business in lid disease at all. Recurrence after apparently adequate curettage should raise the suspicion of meibomian gland carcinoma and prompt biopsy (ophthalmology.pdf p.73). Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Drain a chalazion through the conjunctival (palpebral) surface of the everted lid, keeping skin incisions for anteriorly pointing lesions.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.133, Q9 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: ophthalmology.pdf p.73; L5) Eyelid disease sl.18–19.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-10', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:'A patient with a history of bronchial asthma presents with chronic bilateral conjunctival itching, photophobia, and large cobblestone papillae on the upper tarsal conjunctiva. Which condition is most likely associated?',
    options:['Atopic keratoconjunctivitis','Vernal keratoconjunctivitis','Bacterial conjunctivitis','Trachoma'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'Giant "cobblestone" papillae paving the UPPER tarsal conjunctiva are the signature of vernal keratoconjunctivitis, and the conjunctiva lecture teaches exactly that picture: the everted upper lid carpeted with giant flat-topped tarsal papillae, alongside the limbal form with its chalky white limbal dots and the corneal complications up to the blinding corneal opacity (L6) Conjunctiva, sl.38, 39–45; the deck prints the name "Vernal KeratoConjunctivitis"). VKC is a bilateral, chronic, severely itchy allergic disease of atopic children and young people, and the stem\'s bronchial asthma is the atopic background it keeps company with — asthma and eczema as VKC associations are not taken from the course material, where the deck teaches the entity by photograph. Against atopic keratoconjunctivitis, the discriminators are age and geography of the papillae: AKC is the adult atopic dermatitis-associated disease and favours the LOWER tarsal conjunctiva, without the giant upper-tarsal cobblestones (not taken from the course material). Bacterial conjunctivitis is an acute mucopurulent discharge disease (L6 sl.13–24), not a chronic itching disease with papillary hypertrophy. Trachoma produces FOLLICLES, then pannus, Herbert pits and scarring of the upper tarsus (L6 sl.25–33) — follicular, cicatrising and infective, not papillary and allergic. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Tie giant upper-tarsal cobblestone papillae in an itchy atopic patient to vernal keratoconjunctivitis and separate it from AKC and trachoma.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.134, Q10 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L6) Conjunctiva sl.38, 39–45, 25–33.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-11', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-va',
    stem:'A patient with vision less than 1/60 (only sees hand motion). How do you test the visual field?',
    options:['Automated perimetry','Confrontation visual field test','Light projection test','Amsler grid'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'The examination lecture prints this as a decision tree keyed on acuity: "Confrontation Method → VA = 1/60 or better", "Light Projection → VA less than 1/60", "Amsler Grid → For the central 10°" (L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination). The stem\'s patient sees only hand motion — below 1/60 — so the field is assessed by LIGHT PROJECTION: a light is presented from each quadrant in turn and the patient reports the direction it comes from, mapping which retinal quadrants still function; the deck\'s own slide records the result as good or bad light projection (GLP/BLP) (L1,2 sl.130). Confrontation testing sits one branch up the same tree — comparing the patient\'s field against the examiner\'s finger-counting field face to face — and requires acuity of 1/60 or better, which this patient lacks. Automated perimetry demands steady central fixation on a target the patient must see and respond to threshold stimuli around; an eye that cannot resolve hand movements cannot fixate the machine\'s target at all. The Amsler grid tests only the central 10 degrees of field, one eye at a time at reading distance, and is a macular-function chart for patients with useful central acuity — the AMD monitoring tool of Q27, useless below 1/60. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Choose the field test by acuity: confrontation at 1/60 or better, light projection below 1/60, Amsler grid for the central 10 degrees.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.134, Q11 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination (field-test tree; sl.130 GLP/BLP).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-12', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'A female patient complains of decreased vision. Fundus appears normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
    options:['Vitreous hge','Right optic neuritis','Macular degeneration','Retinal detachment'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'Decreased vision with a completely NORMAL fundus is the signature of retrobulbar neuritis — inflammation of the optic nerve behind the globe, where the ophthalmoscope cannot see it. The book\'s optic neuritis page prints the classification (papillitis; retrobulbar neuritis, flagged "[normal optic disc]") and gives retrobulbar neuritis exactly this clinical picture: acute or subacute vision loss with periocular pain on eye movement, while "The optic disc may appear completely normal", with an afferent pupillary defect, decreased acuity, impaired colour vision and field defects (ophthalmology.pdf p.190). The epidemiology fits the stem\'s one demographic clue: optic neuritis is "Most common in women ages 15 - 45" (p.190), most often demyelinating, with 50% of isolated cases ultimately developing multiple sclerosis. The optic nerve lecture adds the field defect — chronic retrobulbar neuritis produces the centrocecal scotoma (L21) Optic Nerve, sl.4). The bank prints option B as "Right optic neuritis" although the stem states no laterality; the laterality is the bank\'s, kept as printed. Every distractor is excluded by the normal fundus: a vitreous haemorrhage ("Vitreous hge" as printed) obscures the fundus and dims the red reflex; macular degeneration shows drusen and macular change; retinal detachment shows the grey billowing elevated retina. The old clinical aphorism — the patient sees nothing and the doctor sees nothing — is not taken from the course material. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Diagnose retrobulbar neuritis when vision falls behind a normal-looking fundus, and know its young-female, MS-associated profile.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.134, Q12 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: ophthalmology.pdf p.190; L21) Optic Nerve sl.4.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-13', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-va',
    stem:'In which condition does pinhole testing make vision worse?',
    options:['Refractive error','Astigmatism','Macular degeneration','Cataract'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'The pinhole works by optics: it admits only the central bundle of rays, which needs almost no focusing, so blur caused by the eye\'s optical system disappears through it. In any refractive error — myopia, hyperopia and astigmatism included — acuity therefore IMPROVES through a pinhole, which is why it is the standard bedside screen separating optical from organic visual loss; in cataract the effect is variable, often a modest improvement by selecting a clearer lens zone. In MACULAR disease the pinhole does the opposite. The image through a pinhole falls on the fovea, and the pinhole also cuts sharply the total light entering the eye; a degenerate macula is being asked to do all the seeing with less light, while the healthier parafoveal retina the patient may have been using is denied its image — so vision through the pinhole gets WORSE. A pinhole that worsens acuity is thus a pointer to macular pathology. The optics of the pinhole and its behaviour in macular disease are not taken from the course material — no cached ophthalmology lecture or book chapter teaches the pinhole test. This is a concept twin of `opqb-t2-92` ("Vision gets worse after using a pinhole in:", keyed macular edema); the two share the tested fact but every option except the key\'s theme differs — that entry\'s menu keys "Macular edema" where this one keys macular degeneration — so both stand. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Predict that a pinhole improves refractive blur but worsens acuity in macular disease, and use that reversal as a pointer to the macula.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.134, Q13 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1").'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-14', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'A child presents with squint and complains of diplopia. Examination shows one eye +5 D and the other eye +7 D. What is the most appropriate management?',
    options:['Surgery immediately','Patch therapy of the dominant eye','Correction by glasses','Observation only'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'This child has bilateral high hypermetropia with 2 D of anisometropia, and the squinting eye almost certainly carries an accommodative esotropia: the squint lecture prints hypermetropia and a high AC/A ratio as the mechanism of accommodative esotropia, presenting at 2.5–5 years (L29) Sqint I, sl.29), and answers the treatment question with a photograph pair — the same infant esotropic without spectacles and perfectly straight in thick plus-lens spectacles, the deviation fully corrected by the glasses alone (L29 sl.30). Correcting the refractive error is therefore the FIRST step in managing any squint of this profile: full plus correction removes the accommodative drive, often abolishes the deviation, and gives each retina a focused image. The 2 D difference between the eyes is well within what spectacles tolerate — the refraction lecture puts the limit of spectacle correction of anisometropia at about 4 D, beyond which the unequal image sizes force contact lenses or refractive surgery (L7,8) Refractive errors, sl.83, 85). Immediate surgery is wrong because operating before the accommodative component is neutralised misjudges the true angle — surgery is for the residual, non-accommodative deviation (L29 files surgery last, sl.34). Patching treats amblyopia once it is documented; it does not straighten an accommodative squint and is not the first move. Observation alone abandons a child to amblyopia and lost binocularity while both problems are correctable with a pair of glasses. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Correct the refractive error first in a hypermetropic child with squint, reserving patching for amblyopia and surgery for the residual deviation.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.134, Q14 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L29) Sqint I sl.29–30, 34; L7,8) Refractive errors sl.83, 85.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-15', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
    stem:'Imaging done in a diabetic patient with NPDR:',
    options:['MRI','Fundus photography','OCT','Fluorescein angiography'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'The bank keys OCT. Optical coherence tomography gives a cross-sectional scan of the macula and is the imaging that detects and measures diabetic macular oedema — the sight-threatening complication that decides treatment in non-proliferative retinopathy — and the book\'s diabetic retinopathy pages set out both modalities side by side in their imaging tables: fluorescein angiography showing microaneurysms, capillary non-perfusion and leakage, and OCT showing the macular thickening of oedema (ophthalmology.pdf p.241), feeding directly into the treatment of diabetic macular oedema on the next page (p.242). A key-note, recorded and not disputed: fluorescein angiography is also a defensible answer on that same page — it is the classic investigation of the diabetic fundus — and the bank prints no explanation to say why OCT was preferred; the modern practical answer is that OCT is non-invasive, quantitative and the routine test for the macular oedema that matters in NPDR, while FFA is reserved for mapping ischaemia and guiding laser (that ranking is not taken from the course material). Fundus photography documents the retinopathy for grading and screening but images the surface only — it cannot measure oedema. MRI has no role in diabetic retinopathy at all; it is neuro-ophthalmic imaging. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Pair the two imaging tests of diabetic retinopathy — OCT for macular oedema, fluorescein angiography for leakage and non-perfusion.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.134, Q15 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: ophthalmology.pdf p.241–242.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-16', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract-astig',
    stem:'Patient complaining of not seeing near objects but sees far objects clearly. Treatment:',
    options:['Minus lens','Plus lens','Surgery','Observation'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'Clear distance vision with failing near vision is presbyopia — "Poor near vision as an aging process" in the refraction deck\'s own gloss (L7,8) Refractive errors): the crystalline lens hardens with age, accommodation weakens, and the near point recedes beyond reading distance while the distance refraction is untouched. Treatment is the plus lens: a spherical plus (convex) addition over the distance refraction, dispensed as reading spectacles, bifocals or progressives (L7,8 sl.81), the deck\'s correction tree assigning "Convex (Plus lenses)" to hyperopia and presbyopia. A minus (concave) lens is the myopia branch of the same tree and would push the near point further away still. Surgery is not the answer this bank wants for simple presbyopia; the refraction deck reaches for surgical and contact-lens options only where spectacles fail — as in anisometropia beyond the spectacle limit (L7,8 sl.85) — not for routine near-add correction. Observation leaves a fully correctable disability uncorrected. This is the within-section twin of Q3 — the same presbyopia fact behind a partly different option menu (surgery and observation here replace cylindrical glasses and "no treatment is needed"); by the folding rule a replaced distractor makes a new question, so both printings are transcribed. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Recognise presbyopia from failing near vision with clear distance vision and correct it with a plus-lens addition.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.134, Q16 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L7,8) Refractive errors sl.81, correction-tree slide, presbyopia gloss.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-17', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'Congenital glaucoma, commonly associated with:',
    options:['Hyperopia','Myopia','Astigmatism','Presbyopia'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'The infant eye wall is distensible, so raised intraocular pressure does to it what it never does to an adult eye: it stretches it. The glaucoma lecture\'s congenital/developmental glaucoma signs are the record of that stretching — corneal oedema with Haab\'s striae, enlarged corneal diameter, broad limbus, raised IOP, optic nerve cupping and "Increased axial length (A-scan)" (L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry), the buphthalmic "large eye" of the symptom list. An increased axial length IS the anatomy of myopia: the refraction lecture defines axial myopia as an axial length "longer than normal (22-24.5mm)" (L7,8) Refractive errors, sl.42, flagged high-yield), so the stretched, elongated buphthalmic globe is a myopic globe — which is why congenital glaucoma is commonly associated with myopia, and why a documented myopic shift in an infant under treatment is itself a warning that pressure control has been lost (the monitoring point is not taken from the course material). Hyperopia is the opposite error — the SHORT globe (L7,8 axial-length diagram) — and is the refraction of the normal small infant eye, not the buphthalmic one. Astigmatism can follow the corneal scarring of Haab\'s striae but is a secondary corneal effect, not the characteristic association keyed here. Presbyopia is the accommodation failure of middle age and cannot belong to an infant. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Link the increased axial length of buphthalmos to axial myopia as the refractive association of congenital glaucoma.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.134, Q17 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry (signs list); L7,8) Refractive errors sl.42.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-18', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'Child has a refractive error. Doctor instructed to cover one eye for fear of:',
    options:['Strabismus','Cataract','Amblyopia','Glaucoma'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'The fear in a child with a significant refractive error is AMBLYOPIA — a lazy eye. A young visual cortex given a chronically blurred image from one eye stops developing acuity in that eye, and past the sensitive years the loss becomes fixed even if the optics are later corrected; amblyopia is a deficit of visual acuity, not an appearance — nothing on examination of the eye itself shows it. The squint lecture files amblyopia among the sensory adaptations of comitant squint, alongside suppression (L29) Sqint I, sl.20–23), printing the name over a clinical photograph without defining it. The doctor\'s instruction is the standard defence: OCCLUSION — covering the better eye to force the child to use and develop the amblyopic one — is printed as the treatment: "Occlusion — Treatment of amblyopia", shown with both the opaque fabric occluder and the translucent frosted patch over spectacles (L29 sl.32). The deck never defines amblyopia or gives an age limit; the critical-period mechanism above is not taken from the course material. Strabismus is a cause and companion of amblyopia, not what patching guards against — indeed occlusion is prescribed in squint precisely because the squint threatens amblyopia. Cataract in a child causes deprivation amblyopia but is not produced by a refractive error, and glaucoma has no connection to patching at all. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Name amblyopia as the danger in a child\'s uncorrected refractive error and occlusion of the better eye as its treatment.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.134, Q18 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L29) Sqint I sl.20–23, 32.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-19', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'Patient with CRVO associated with macular edema. Treatment:',
    options:['Topical steroid','Anti-VEGF injection','Oral acetazolamide','Laser trabeculoplasty'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'Macular oedema after CRVO is fluid leakage driven by the occluded, hypoxic retina, and the hypoxic retina\'s signal is vascular endothelial growth factor — so intravitreal anti-VEGF injection is the treatment, suppressing the leakage and drying the macula over repeated injections. The retinal vascular lecture names chronic macular oedema among the features of non-ischaemic CRVO and, in its vein-occlusion pages, manages chronic macular oedema by observation, angiography and selective laser (L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders) — it never prints anti-VEGF; the book brings anti-VEGF therapy into the module only for diabetic macular oedema, in its DME treatment scheme alongside focal laser (ophthalmology.pdf p.242). The specific indication of anti-VEGF for CRVO-associated macular oedema — now the first-line treatment — is not taken from the course material. Topical steroid drops do not reach the macula in therapeutic concentration; the steroid route that works in CRVO oedema is an intravitreal implant, which is not what option A says. Oral acetazolamide is the treatment of raised intraocular pressure and of some special maculopathies, not of vein-occlusion oedema. Laser trabeculoplasty treats open-angle glaucoma at the trabecular meshwork; its only kinship with CRVO is that ischaemic CRVO can later cause NEOVASCULAR glaucoma — which is treated with panretinal photocoagulation, a different laser to a different target (L19.2). Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Treat the macular oedema of CRVO with intravitreal anti-VEGF and keep laser for ischaemic complications, not the oedema.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.134, Q19 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders; ophthalmology.pdf p.242.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-20', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'In case presents with tear drop sign on imaging, initial treatment:',
    options:['Surgical repair','IV antibiotics','Observation','Topical steroids'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'The teardrop sign is the radiological signature of an orbital floor blow-out fracture: the trauma lecture prints it as "Tissue entrapment within the fracture (TEAR DROP SIGN)" — orbital fat and inferior rectus/inferior oblique tissue herniated through the fractured floor, hanging into the maxillary sinus as a teardrop-shaped soft-tissue mass on the coronal image (L25) Trauma I, sl.23). The bank keys IV antibiotics, and the lecture\'s own management slide supports antibiotics early in the sequence: the blow-out fracture management is to refrain from nose-blowing, give SYSTEMIC ANTIBIOTICS, and proceed to surgery when required (L25 sl.24, flagged core) — the fracture opens the orbit into an unsterile paranasal sinus, so prophylactic systemic antibiotics guard against orbital cellulitis, and the nose-blowing ban prevents sinus air being forced into the orbit. The lecture prints the route as "systemic" without specifying intravenous; the IV route is the bank\'s. Surgical repair is deliberately NOT initial: it is reserved for the fracture that declares a need — persistent diplopia from entrapment, significant enophthalmos, a large floor defect — and even then is usually done after the swelling settles (the operative indications are not taken from the course material beyond the lecture\'s "surgery when required"). Observation alone would be defensible for an uncomplicated fracture but is not what the printed key or the lecture\'s antibiotic step says. Topical steroids treat surface inflammation and do nothing for a fractured orbital floor. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Read the teardrop sign as a blow-out fracture with tissue entrapment and start systemic antibiotics with a nose-blowing ban, operating only when required.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.134, Q20 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L25) Trauma I sl.23–24.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-21', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'(III) palsy picture. Which muscle is spared?',
    options:['Medial rectus','Superior rectus','Lateral rectus','Inferior oblique'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'The third (oculomotor) nerve supplies four of the six extraocular muscles — medial rectus, superior rectus, inferior rectus and inferior oblique — plus levator palpebrae and the pupil\'s sphincter; the two survivors of a complete third nerve palsy are the LATERAL RECTUS (sixth nerve) and the superior oblique (fourth nerve). Of the four muscles offered, only the lateral rectus is spared. The palsy\'s classic picture proves it: the squint lecture shows the ptotic lid lifted to reveal an eye sitting DOWN AND OUT (L30) Squint II, sl.11–12) — it sits OUT precisely because the intact lateral rectus, unopposed by the paralysed medial rectus, drags the globe into abduction, and DOWN because the intact superior oblique depresses it against the lost elevators. An eye whose lateral rectus were also paralysed could not be pulled out. The same lecture adds the pupil rule — pupil involved means compression, pupil spared means vascular, because the pupillary fibres run on the OUTSIDE of the nerve (L30 sl.13) — and the book pairs the third nerve palsy with the sixth nerve (abducent) palsy, whose sole muscle is this same lateral rectus (ophthalmology.pdf p.184). Medial rectus, superior rectus and inferior oblique are all third-nerve muscles and all paralysed; each distractor names a casualty, not a survivor. The innervation table itself (LR6 SO4, the rest 3) is not taken from the course material as a printed list. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Derive the down-and-out eye of third nerve palsy from its two spared muscles — lateral rectus and superior oblique.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.135, Q21 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L30) Squint II sl.11–13; ophthalmology.pdf p.184.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-22', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:'In corneal abrasion. Patient will complain of:',
    options:['Double vision','Photophobia','Color blindness','Pain-free vision'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'The cornea is the most densely innervated surface of the body, and stripping its epithelium exposes those nerve endings: a corneal abrasion produces severe pain, PHOTOPHOBIA, tearing, blepharospasm and blurring. The cornea lecture prints exactly this symptom set for the epithelial-defect diseases of the cornea — "Pain, photophobia, blurred vision" head the symptom list for keratitis (L15,16) Cornea) — and the examination lecture files corneal abrasion in its red-eye differential table on the painful side, in the same column as keratitis and corneal foreign body (L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination). The photophobia is reflex: the exposed nerve endings drive ciliary spasm, and light-triggered pupillary movement grinds the inflamed iris and ciliary body — which is why cycloplegic drops relieve the pain of corneal disease (L15,16, cycloplegics "reduce pain from ciliary spasm"). Double vision is a motility or media problem, not an epithelial one — an abrasion moves no muscle. Colour blindness is an optic nerve and macular symptom (impaired colour vision belongs to optic neuritis, ophthalmology.pdf p.190), nowhere near the corneal surface. "Pain-free vision" is the exact opposite of the presentation — a painLESS corneal picture should make the examiner think of neurotrophic disease, where sensation itself is lost, not of an abrasion. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Expect pain, photophobia and tearing from a corneal abrasion, and let painlessness argue against it.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.135, Q22 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L15,16) Cornea (keratitis symptoms; cycloplegia for ciliary spasm); L1,2) red-eye table; ophthalmology.pdf p.190.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-23', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'Patient injured by a hammer, positive fluorescein test will show:',
    options:['Red staining','Clearness of dye','Green fluorescein uptake','No change'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'A hammer injury is the trauma lecture\'s own example of a suspicious history for an open-globe injury — its rule-out slide lists "Suspicious history: e.g. HAMMERING", with peaked pupil, abnormal anterior chamber depth and bullous subconjunctival haemorrhage among the warning signs, and closes with the test this question is about: "A positive SEIDEL TEST will help detect leaking aqueous" (L25) Trauma I, sl.49–50). The Seidel test is fluorescein used differently from routine staining: concentrated dye is painted over the suspect site, and if the globe is breached, aqueous humour streams out through the wound and washes a channel through the dye — the leak shows as a CLEARING, a diluted, flowing rivulet cutting through the dark concentrated fluorescein, which is what the bank\'s key "clearness of dye" describes (the mechanics of the dilution stream are not taken from the course material; the lecture prints the test\'s name and purpose). That is why B is keyed over C: "green fluorescein uptake" describes the ORDINARY positive of surface staining — an abrasion or ulcer bed taking up dye and glowing green under blue light (fluorescein "stains the ulcer bed", L15,16) Cornea) — a closed-globe finding, not the sign of a perforation. Red staining is not a behaviour fluorescein has, and "no change" is a negative Seidel test — the reassuring result, not the positive one the stem asks for. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Distinguish the Seidel positive — aqueous clearing a stream through concentrated fluorescein at a leak — from ordinary green surface staining.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.135, Q23 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L25) Trauma I sl.49–50; L15,16) Cornea.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-24', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'Patient with cataract. What is a possible complication?',
    options:['Glaucoma','Corneal opacity','Retinal detachment','Optic neuritis'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:'A cataract can raise the pressure of its own eye, and the cataract lecture prints the mechanism chain: among the complications of cataract are INTUMESCENCE — "lens fibres liquefaction and fluid imbibition → swelling of lens" — and LENS-INDUCED GLAUCOMA in two forms: PHACOMORPHIC, where the intumescent lens\'s increased thickness produces pupil block and elevated intraocular pressure, and PHACOLYTIC (L17) Cataract I). Phacomorphic glaucoma is angle closure by a swollen lens; phacolytic glaucoma is the hypermature cataract\'s liquefied lens proteins leaking through an intact capsule and clogging the trabecular meshwork with protein-laden macrophages — the leakage mechanism beyond the lecture\'s printed word "Phacolytic" is not taken from the course material. So glaucoma is a directly printed complication of cataract, and the key. Corneal opacity is not caused by cataract — the two are separate media opacities, and a hazy cornea in this context should suggest another diagnosis (the L12 congenital-glaucoma differential even lists traumatic corneal oedema separately). Retinal detachment is a complication of high myopia, trauma and cataract SURGERY, not of the unoperated cataract itself. Optic neuritis is inflammatory demyelination of the optic nerve (ophthalmology.pdf p.190) with no causal link to lens opacity. Note the direction of causation runs both ways in this module: complicated cataract is cataract caused by other eye disease, while this question asks what cataract causes. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Name lens-induced glaucoma — phacomorphic pupil block and phacolytic meshwork obstruction — as the complication of a swelling or hypermature cataract.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.135, Q24 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L17) Cataract I (complications: intumescence, lens-induced glaucoma, phacomorphic, phacolytic).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-25', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit',
    stem:'In orbital cellulitis. Possible complication is:',
    options:['Retinal detachment','Brain abscess','Glaucoma','Corneal ulcer'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'Orbital cellulitis is infection BEHIND the orbital septum, and the orbit has no barrier worth the name between itself and the cranial cavity — the orbital veins are valveless and drain backward through the cavernous sinus. The orbit lecture\'s complications slide covers both territories: ocular complications include optic neuropathy, exposure keratopathy, raised IOP, endophthalmitis and occlusion of the central retinal artery or vein; subperiosteal abscess forms most frequently along the MEDIAL orbital wall (the ethmoid is the usual source sinus); and the intracranial complications, "uncommon but extremely serious", are meningitis, BRAIN ABSCESS and cavernous sinus thrombosis (L10) orbit, sl.27 — the cached slide\'s intracranial list is cut off in the original and reconstructed there; the entities named are the standard triad). Brain abscess is therefore a printed possible complication and the key — and the reason orbital cellulitis is treated as an emergency with intravenous antibiotics continued until four days apyrexial, optic nerve monitoring, and early drainage of any orbital abscess (L10 management slide). Retinal detachment is not among the printed complications — even the retinal complications listed are VASCULAR occlusions, not detachment. Raised IOP is listed, but "glaucoma" as a disease is not the recorded outcome, and it is in any case an ocular nuisance beside an intracranial abscess. Corneal ulcer overstates the printed "exposure keratopathy" — proptosis exposing the cornea — which is surface damage from exposure, not the keyed complication. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'List the intracranial dangers of orbital cellulitis — meningitis, brain abscess, cavernous sinus thrombosis — behind its ocular complications.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.135, Q25 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L10) orbit sl.27 and management slide.'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-26', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'In Central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO). Emergency treatment is:',
    options:['IV mannitol','Oral acetazolamide','Intravitreal steroid','Laser photocoagulation'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:'The logic of emergency CRAO treatment is hydraulic: drop the intraocular pressure as fast as possible so that the perfusion pressure behind the obstruction rises and the embolus is pushed on into a smaller branch. Of the pressure-lowering options offered, IV MANNITOL is the fastest — an intravenous osmotic agent that dehydrates the vitreous within minutes — and it is the keyed emergency drug. The module prints IV mannitol as its rapid ocular hypotensive in the treatment of acute angle-closure glaucoma (L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry); its application to CRAO is not taken from the course material — the acute-visual-loss lecture\'s CRAO management slides are blank in the cached deck, which records the absence. Oral acetazolamide lowers pressure by the same intent but more slowly and less profoundly; in practice it is given alongside mannitol, but as the single emergency agent it is second best, and that comparative ranking is likewise not taken from the course material. Intravitreal steroid has no target — an infarcting retina is not an inflammatory or oedematous one in the treatable sense — and injecting volume into the eye acutely RAISES the pressure, the exact opposite of the goal. Laser photocoagulation treats neovascularisation and retinal breaks; it cannot move an embolus and has no role in the acute event. This question is the pharmacological companion of Q8, whose keyed first manoeuvre for the same diagnosis is ocular massage. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Lower intraocular pressure fast in CRAO — IV mannitol as the emergency osmotic agent — to drive the embolus downstream.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.135, Q26 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry (IV mannitol); L31,32) Acute visual Loss (CRAO management slides blank in the cached deck).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-27', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
    stem:'How to monitor progression of Age-related macular degeneration?',
    options:['Visual acuity chart','Amsler grid','Slit-lamp exam','Retinoscopy'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'The AMSLER GRID is the macular chart: a square grid of straight lines with a central fixation dot, viewed one eye at a time at reading distance, testing the central 10 degrees of field — the examination lecture\'s test tree assigns it exactly that territory, "Amsler Grid → For the central 10°" (L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination), which is the macula\'s territory. The chronic-visual-loss lecture lists it (printed "Amsilar grid" — the deck\'s own spelling) among the tests of macular function, beside acuity, pupillary reaction, the photostress test and fluorescein angiography, and its AMD pages print the symptoms the grid is built to catch: micropsia, macropsia, METAMORPHOPSIA and scotoma (L35,36) Chronic visual loss). Monitoring works because progression of AMD — above all the conversion from dry to wet disease — announces itself as new distortion: straight grid lines turn wavy or a patch of the grid drops out, and the patient checking the grid at home can report the change within days, when treatment of a new neovascular membrane matters most (the home-use protocol is not taken from the course material). A visual acuity chart measures foveal resolution only and moves late and coarsely. The slit lamp examines the anterior segment; the macula needs a fundus lens added and in any case biomicroscopy is the clinic\'s tool, not the patient\'s monitor. Retinoscopy measures refraction and says nothing about the macula. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Use the Amsler grid — the central 10 degrees, one eye at a time — to catch the new metamorphopsia of progressing AMD.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.135, Q27 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L1,2) field-test tree; L35,36) Chronic visual loss (macular function tests; AMD symptoms).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-28', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'Patient with chemical eye burn. One of the acute signs is:',
    options:['Limbus ischemia','Dry eye','Cataract','Retinal detachment'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:'Limbal ischaemia is both an acute sign and THE prognostic sign of a chemical burn. The trauma lecture\'s staging puts limbal blanching in the IMMEDIATE ACUTE PHASE (first 3 days): conjunctival epithelial necrosis, chemosis and LIMBAL BLANCHING, with corneal epithelial necrosis, oedema and opacification, and a pressure rise from collagen shrinkage (L26) Trauma II). The same lecture grades severity by it: Grade I — clear cornea, limbal ischaemia nil, excellent prognosis; Grade II — ischaemia less than one-third; Grade III — one-third to one-half, guarded prognosis; Grade IV — more than one-half, very poor prognosis (L26 grading table). The limbus matters because it houses the corneal epithelial stem cells: a blanched, infarcted limbus cannot re-epithelialise the cornea, which is why the extent of the white, avascular limbus predicts the eye\'s future (the stem-cell rationale is not taken from the course material). Alkalis are the worst offenders — they combine with membrane lipids and stromal mucopolysaccharides and penetrate deep, where acids coagulate surface proteins and self-limit (L26). Every distractor is a LATE consequence, not an acute sign: dry eye follows the cicatricial phase\'s conjunctival scarring and goblet-cell loss (late phase, beyond 7 days), cataract follows deep penetration of alkali over time, and retinal detachment is not a printed feature of chemical injury at any stage. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Recognise limbal ischaemia as the acute sign that grades a chemical burn and predicts corneal healing.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.135, Q28 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L26) Trauma II (acute phase; grading of severity; alkali vs acid).'
  },
  {
    id:'opqb-t21-29', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'Treatment of retinal detachment includes:',
    options:['Topical steroid','Scleral buckle','LASIK','Trabeculectomy'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment is a surgical disease, and the retinal detachment lecture prints its three operations: VITRECTOMY, SCLERAL BUCKLING — "a silicone band" sutured onto the sclera to indent the eye wall inward against the retinal break, closing the tear and relieving vitreous traction — and PNEUMATIC RETINOPEXY, an expanding gas bubble injected into the vitreous to tamponade the break from inside (L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations, sl.14). Scleral buckle is the one of the three offered here, and the key. The buckle works mechanically: the indentation brings the detached retina back against the pigment epithelium and the break is then sealed with cryotherapy or laser around it, the sealing step being standard practice not printed on the slide (not taken from the course material). Topical steroid drops treat surface and anterior segment inflammation; no drop reattaches a retina, and the only detachment type managed medically is the EXUDATIVE form, where the underlying inflammatory or vascular disease is treated — a systemic strategy, not a topical one (L20 sl.8). LASIK is corneal refractive surgery; worth noting the direction of risk actually runs the other way — the highly myopic eyes that seek refractive surgery are the same long eyes predisposed to detachment (L20 sl.6 lists high myopia among causes). Trabeculectomy is glaucoma filtration surgery, lowering intraocular pressure through a scleral fistula; it has no retinal action at all. Written for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'List the three operations for rhegmatogenous detachment — vitrectomy, scleral buckle, pneumatic retinopexy — and reject medical and refractive distractors.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.135, Q29 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 1"). Supporting: L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations sl.6, 8, 14.'
  }